idget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/state.json" "file_sha256" "284e7ca9146e141d4785aec462536df2a2b7db0a282f39f8675e2fbbfef157d9"} {"question" "q-331052" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "F(s)"] ["math" "\\frac{F(s)}{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{F(s)}"] ["math" "sF(s)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/state.json" "file_sha256" "284e7ca9146e141d4785aec462536df2a2b7db0a282f39f8675e2fbbfef157d9"} {"question" "q-331052" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{F(s)}{s}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/state.json" "file_sha256" "284e7ca9146e141d4785aec462536df2a2b7db0a282f39f8675e2fbbfef157d9"} {"question" "e-21503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\}  = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$  find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/example-21503.json" "file_sha256" "dd36e04a173914a96f235ae052e87558a5f9202b91121d0205915df0d3582375"} {"question" "e-21503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t) = \\cos(\\pi t).$ Thus, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/example-21503.json" "file_sha256" "dd36e04a173914a96f235ae052e87558a5f9202b91121d0205915df0d3582375"} {"question" "e-21527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/example-21527.json" "file_sha256" "dc74dd31f89fb6a0a6585644f90123f5b8799d5dbfcb631c7ef29c1c6ba8f11a"} {"question" "e-21527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/example-21527.json" "file_sha256" "dc74dd31f89fb6a0a6585644f90123f5b8799d5dbfcb631c7ef29c1c6ba8f11a"} {"question" "e-21551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/example-21551.json" "file_sha256" "6e45d4efe35db1f542160b13ab9b9c23ebc91cdb8450f7cd1d9b7026cd2513c9"} {"question" "e-21551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/example-21551.json" "file_sha256" "6e45d4efe35db1f542160b13ab9b9c23ebc91cdb8450f7cd1d9b7026cd2513c9"}] "usage" {"e-21527" [] "e-21503" [] "e-21551" [] "q-331052" []} "basis" 2698 "retractions" [[17592186221880 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L{∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ}$ given $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A^{′}(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) = 0$. Then,\n\n$f(τ)$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186221098 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0$."] [17592186221098 :question/worked-solution "We know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos(\\pi t)\n$$\n\nThus, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= L{f(t)} \\\\\n&= L{\\cos (πt)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{∫_{0}^{t}\\cos (πτ)dτ} \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s^{2} + π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221099 :question/problem "For the function\n$G(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a$."] [17592186221099 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{F(s)} &= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} \\\\\n&= e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{G(s)} &= L^{-1}{(F(s))/(s)} \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}e^{τ}dτ \\\\\n&= e^{τ} \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= e^{t} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221104 :question/problem "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}$."] [17592186221104 :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nand $A(0) ={0}$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ &= A(τ) \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= A(t) - A(0) \\\\\n&= A(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ} &= L{A(t)} = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = A(t) & ⇒ & du = A^{′}(t)dt \\\\ dv = e^{-st}dt & ⇒ & v =-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt &= A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}) \\mid _{0}^{∞} - ∫_{0}^{∞}(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})A^{′}(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg \\mid _0^{\\infty} = {0}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{s}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A^{′}(t)dt &= \\frac{1}{s}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot F(s) \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220182 17592186220183 17592186220184 17592186220185] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220181 :answer-field/id] [17592186220181 :answer-field/key] [17592186220181 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
